powerpc/powernv: add pnv_npu2_destroy_pasid() for nest-MMU context teardown

The driver-mode ATS implementation in NVIDIA's nvidia-uvm (used on kernels
>= 5.9, where the in-kernel NPU2 driver was removed) never tears down the
nest-MMU translation context for a process: the nest MMU arms a context on
demand when the first translation for a PASID happens, and when the process
exits nothing destroys it. The nest MMU then keeps the dead PASID's context
and TLB entries, GPU memory attributed to the process is not freed
(nvidia-smi keeps reporting it as in use), and a recycled PID can hit stale
translations.

On 4.18 this teardown happened in-kernel: pnv_npu2_destroy_context()
issued opal_npu_destroy_context() for the process's PASID as the last step
of GPU VA space unregistration. This patch restores an equivalent,
minimal, per-PASID entry point:

  pnv_npu2_destroy_pasid(gpdev, pasid)

    - finds the GPU's NPU brick with pnv_pci_get_npu_dev() (patch 0001)
    - rejects non-NPU2 PHBs via the "ibm,ioda2-npu2-phb" compatible,
      exactly as pnv_npu2_map_lpar_dev()/pnv_npu2_unmap_lpar_dev() do
    - issues opal_npu_destroy_context(opal_id, pasid, PCI_DEVID(gpu))

The TLB flush itself is deliberately NOT done here. In 4.18 the kernel's
mmu_notifier .release issued the full-PASID ATSD at exit_mmap, and the OPAL
destroy ran afterwards, in the driver's unregister path. The matching
driver patch (driver/0002-uvm-ats-exit-teardown-ppc64le.patch) restores the
same ordering in driver mode: UVM's new mmu_notifier .release performs the
ATSD flush, and uvm_ats_ibm_unregister_gpu_va_space_driver() calls
pnv_npu2_destroy_pasid() as the last teardown step.

The symbol is exported non-GPL (EXPORT_SYMBOL) because the only consumer is
NVIDIA's out-of-tree nvidia-uvm module, which is not GPL and therefore
cannot link EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL symbols. This is the same reason
pnv_pci_get_npu_dev() and pnv_pci_get_gpu_dev() are plain exports.

Apply order: 0001 -> 0002 -> 0003 -> 0004. All hunks require 0003 applied
first (the npu-dma.c hunk anchors on the unmap function 0003 adds, and the
header hunks on 0003's declarations).

Risk: LOW. Dead code plus one exported symbol until the matching driver
patch is built; cannot change boot behaviour. Worst case on a firmware that
dislikes the OPAL call is a dev_err() line per teardown.

Derived from: v4.18 arch/powerpc/platforms/powernv/npu-dma.c
pnv_npu2_destroy_context() (OPAL call sequence) and the v5.12 unmap
structure restored by patch 0003.

---
NOTE (2026-10-04): nothing calls this any more. nvidia-uvm called it once per process exit, and because
OPAL's NPU context is per GPU (armed once at boot, PASID argument unused) that removed the GPU's whole
translation context: the next CUDA context on that GPU hung. The driver patch in nvidia-power9-v100 dropped the
call. Kept only because the kernel that was boot-tested carried it; a candidate for removal.

